Bob,

Pardon the delay in my reply. I've been traveling.

In order to test Stan, I've tried to reproduce the marginal for a 3D simplex. First, to confirm that the beta is indeed the marginal of the Dirichlet, I marginalized the Dirichlet([1 1 1]) distribution in Mathematica with the following result:

   In:     Integrate[PDF[DirichletDistribution[{1, 1, 1}], {x1, x2}], {x2, 0, 1 - x1}, Assumptions -> {0 < x1 < 1}]
   Out: 2(1 - x1)

This agrees with the beta distribution, as you said:

   In:    PDF[BetaDistribution[1, 2], x1]
   Out: 2(1 - x1)

So far, so good. Then I simulated 10^6 samples in Stan using

parameters {
    simplex[3] x;
}
model {

    // x ~ normal(0,1);
}

Note that I've commented out the contribution of the normal distribution this time. A histogram of the results is given here (Using this link <https://drive.google.com/open?id=0ByZrPY6DK0cAaFFwYnRyckNxZlk> if the image doesn't appear):

 <https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-Dx8UWfm4jdc/WCB_Qjv0qoI/AAAAAAAAeQ4/Eo0qnSES8YYs7OpkI_1aOjYZF4GYmWBkACLcB/s1600/Simplex%2Bmarginal.png>

You'll note that the histogram differs from the beta distribution for x1 < 0.1. This would explain why my simplex samples on a unit normal are wrong, given that the marginal of the simplex samples themselves is wrong.

Have I discovered a bug in Stan? Much of my research involves compositional distributions, and I rely heavily on Stan, so I'm keen to resolve this issue.

Michael

