> On Nov 13, 2016, at 3:36 AM, stefano <mangiol...@gmail.com> wrote:
>

> 2016-11-13 19:22 GMT+11:00 stefano <mangiol...@gmail.com>:
>
> 2016-11-13 13:26 GMT+11:00 Bob Carpenter <ca...@alias-i.com>:
>   theta1 drawn from ~ lognormal(mu1, sigma1)
>   theta2 drawn from ~ lognormal(mu2, sigma2)
>   theta3 drawn from ~ lognormal(mu3, sigma3)
>
>   y[n] = (theta1 * phi[n,1]) + (theta2 * phi[n,2]) + (theta3 * phi[n,3])
>
> If y and phi are both known, then it gets tricky.  You
> only have two degrees of freedom, and given that the theta
> are positive (they're lognormal), you have
>
>   0  <  theta[n, 1] * phi[n, 1]  <  y[n]
>
> and then
>
>   0  < theta[n, 2] * phi[n, 2] < y[n] - theta[n, 1] * phi[n, 1]
>
> and then
>
>  theta3[n] = y[n] - (theta1[n] * phi[n, 1]) + (theta2[n] * phi[n, 2]);
>
> You have to do those two constraints custom, define theta3 as
> a transformed parameter, and then do the Jacobian adjustments.
>
> Possible, but a pain to code.
>
> I see your point.
>
> Let's start with saying that this is a last step of a method that already outperformed a one-year-old Stanford-made alternative (Nature methods), and I will do whatever it takes to complete and publish this high impact advance and paper.
>
> So if it will be painful to code, well I will do it (maybe with some help :) )
>
> Said that I will raise 3 points:
>
> = 1 ==================================================
>
> What if I say that actually, phi is a simplex for which
>
> y[n] = ( (theta1 * phi[n,1]) + (theta2 * phi[n,2]) + (theta3 * phi[n,3]) ) + theta_background * phi_background
>
> sorry I correct
>
> y[n] = ( (theta1 * phi[n,1]) + (theta2 * phi[n,2]) + (theta3 * phi[n,3]) ) * (1-phi_background) + theta_background * phi_background
>
>
> Does this make any difference?

That'd just be another way of adding noise, right?  And the
phi_background or theta_background would vary with n?

- Bob

