Date: Mon, 21 Nov 2016 16:12:19 +0000
Subject: Slow Computation
Message-ID: <JQgiDzCJpno.PdlWp_uXBAAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/JQgiDzCJpno/m/PdlWp_uXBAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I am trying to run a simple two-stage hierarchical model in which the sum of multipliers for each child comes from a common distribution (see stan code below), with 100 children (F), 6 lags (K), and 40 to 50 data points for each child (D ~ 100). STAN takes about an hour to finish. I am wondering if this is acceptable run time or my for loops is inefficiently coded.

regards,
Vishy

data {
  int<lower=1> N; // total number of observations
  int<lower=1> D; // number of quarters/months/days
  int<lower=1> K; // number of predictors (factors / lags)
  int<lower=1> F; // number of children

  int start[F];
  int sizes[F];

  matrix[D, K] X; // predictor matrix
  vector[N] y; // observables
}
parameters {
  vector[F] alpha; // intercept
  matrix[K, F] fb; // multipliers to different lags
  vector<lower=0>[F] e; // child level errors

  real gb; // group Beta
  real<lower=0> sigma; // parent level errors
}
transformed parameters {
  vector[F] sum_beta;
  for(i in 1:F) {
    sum_beta[i] = sum(col(fb, i));
  }
}
model {
  int st;
  int end;
  for(i in 1:F) {
    st = start[i];
    end = st + sizes[i] - 1;
    segment(y, st, sizes[i]) ~ student_t(sizes[i] - 1, alpha[i] + X[st:end,] * col(fb, i), e[i]); // child level regression
    sum_beta[i] ~ normal(gb, sigma); // common parent distribution
  }

  // hyperpriors
  gb ~ normal(1, 10);
  sigma ~ cauchy(0, 2);
  e ~ cauchy(0, 2);
}

From u4e90fa9f1b035969 at groups.google.com  Mon Nov 21 16:39:54 2016
From: u4e90fa9f1b035969 at groups.google.com (Maxwell Joseph)
Date: Mon, 21 Nov 2016 16:39:54 +0000
Subject: Re: Identifiability in Exploratory Factor Analysis
Message-ID: <1jh0pdZgnDs.oCPH43yZBAAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <1jh0pdZgnDs.qrdaENpkBAAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <1jh0pdZgnDs.VTdUIOxdBAAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com> <1jh0pdZgnDs.qrdaENpkBAAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/1jh0pdZgnDs/m/oCPH43yZBAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

You might check out this recent paper by Leung & Drton, who devise priors for the loading matrix that they claim leads to an identified model: http://arxiv.org/pdf/1409.7672

Here's a little simulation and a rough translation to Stan syntax (though some of my priors are different): https://gist.github.com/mbjoseph/952b807bf5aad4a72a9d865f84d67afa

It's a little more complicated than a typical EFA because I have coded it to accommodate and estimate missing observations.

Maybe useful?

On Sunday, November 20, 2016 at 5:35:20 PM UTC-7, Stephen Martin wrote:
> Under this formulation, I don't think there is a way to address the multimodality. Honestly, I don't think EFA is generally identified. It extracts factors and labels them in descending order of eigenvalue. It then just applies a transform to the eigenvectors to produce a simple structure. I'm also willing to bet that if you ran an EFA multiple times, you may get similar loadings, but with permuted latent variable names.
>
> One could try imposing prior constraints where, e.g., loading from 'factor 1' to item 1 puts most of the probability above 0, centered, e.g., at 1, whereas the loading to the same item to the other factors is spiked at 0 with some probability above and below. However, I'm guessing this would only identify one factor and its loadings with other items; the other K-1 factors would probably swap arbitrarily.
>
> I'd be interested in solutions to this too, but I'm guessing there isn't one.
>
