Hi,

I am trying to fit a model in Stan where the number of times a coin is flipped in each replicate of an experiment is N (which is constant across all experimental replicates). This is being used as a tutorial example to teach people how to code up discrete parameter models in Stan. Suppose I have data on the number of heads obtained across 10 replicates of the experiment Y <- c(9,7,11,10,10,9,8,11,9,11), and specify theta ~ beta(7,2) for the probability of the coin landing heads up. Also I am assuming that all values of N between 11 and 20 (included) are equally likely a priori.

As far as I can tell, the model below should work. However, I am getting quite different answers from a Gibbs sampler I have also coded. In particular, the theta parameter is estimated to be about 0.78 (close to its prior mean) via Gibbs, and about 0.64 via NUTS using the below. I suspect that the Gibbs answer is correct, because I don't expect the data to be that informative about the parameter's value (although I suppose the variance of the data gives you something). However, am happy to be proved wrong!

Can someone confirm with me that the below is correct or tell me why it isn't? Best, Ben

data{
  int K;
  int Y[K];
}

transformed data{
  int N[10];
  for(s in 1:10){
    N[s] = 10 + s;
  }
}

parameters{
  real<lower=0,upper=1> theta;
}

transformed parameters{
  matrix[K,10] lp;

  for(i in 1:K){
    for(s in 1:10){
      lp[i,s] = log(0.1) + binomial_lpmf(Y[i]|N[s],theta);
    }
  }
}

model{
  for(i in 1:K){
    target += log_sum_exp(lp[i]);
  }
  theta ~ beta(7,2);
}

generated quantities{
  real pState[10];
  for(s in 1:10)
    pState[s] = exp(log_sum_exp(col(lp,s)))/exp(log_sum_exp(lp));
}

