I'm getting unexpected behavior with syntax with a custom lpdf function. If the issue is there's an actual bug, I'll create a MWE and submit it on Github. If it's just that I misunderstand the syntax.

Consider two functions:

> real lower_truncated_normal_lpdf(real x, real mu, real sigma, real A) {
>     return(normal_lpdf(x | mu, sigma) - normal_lccdf(A | mu, sigma));
> } // This function isn't always necessary, since sometimes we can use Stan's built-in syntax of normal(x|mu, sigma) T[A,], but sometimes it is still needed
>
> real lower_truncated_normal_lpdf_vector(vector x, real mu, real sigma, real A) {
>     return(normal_lpdf(x | mu, sigma) - normal_lccdf(A | mu, sigma));
> } // As the first function, but assume the input is a vector.

The issue is in calling them:

> lower_truncated_normal_lpdf(x[t] | mu, sigma, 0) //x[t] is a scalar. This runs.
>
> lower_truncated_normal_lpdf_vector(x | mu, sigma, 0) //x is a vector. This fails with a parser error.
>
> lower_truncated_normal_lpdf_vector(x, mu, sigma, 0) //x is a vector. This runs.

I can understand why the second one fails but the third runs. But what I don't understand is why the first then runs.

Here's the exact parser error:
> target += lower_truncated_normal_lpdf_vector(x | mu, sigma, 0);
>                                             ^
> PARSER EXPECTED: "("

From ucab04b8f0759c712 at groups.google.com  Wed Nov 30 19:09:25 2016
